+        <mxCell id="55TKrNyZNPNLfdjvmu9W-365" value="0. Content Updates (CU) contain all double-buffered state of the surface and selected state from their direct children.&#xa;&#xa;1. Surfaces which are effectively synchronized create Synchronized Content Updates (SCU), otherwise they create Desync Content Updates (DCU).&#xa;&#xa;2. When a CU is created, it gets a dependency on the previous CU of the same queues (if it exists).&#xa;&#xa;3. When a CU is created, it gets a dependency on the last SCU of direct child surfaces that are not reachable (if they exists).&#xa;&#xa;4. The CUs and their dependencies form a DAG, where CUs are nodes and dependencies are edges.&#xa;&#xa;5. All DCUs starting from the front of the queues until the first SCU or the back of the queue is reached are candidates.&#xa;&#xa;6. If the DAG that&#39;s reachable from a candidate (candidate DAG) does not have any constraints, then this DAG can be applied.&#xa;&#xa;7. A DAG gets applied atomically by recursively applying a content update without dependencies and removing it from the DAG.&#xa;&#xa;8. Surfaces transition from effectively sync to effectively desync after their parents.&#xa;&#xa;9. When a surface transitions to effectively desync, all SCUs in its queue which are not reachable by a DCU become DCUs.&#xa;" style="text;whiteSpace=wrap;fontSize=16;labelPosition=center;verticalLabelPosition=middle;align=left;verticalAlign=top;movable=1;resizable=1;rotatable=1;deletable=1;editable=1;locked=0;connectable=1;" parent="55TKrNyZNPNLfdjvmu9W-381" vertex="1">
